3.53.m:
3.264
Haṃsa said:
A man endowed with self-control will attain all desires;
therefore, self-control should be exercised with all effort by one who knows.
Subjugation of the senses is called self-control in this world;
no rites of one who is not self-controlled are fruitful here, O best of twice-born.
One who is not self-controlled does not attain success, nor a desired destination;
one who is not self-controlled does not attain fortune, nor indeed anything at all.
Of a mortal with five senses, if even a single sense is breached,
then his wisdom leaks away, like water from a leather vessel.
What wise man would abandon the aims of human life and constantly strive
for the sake of the senses, when striving for his own true interest?
Through attachment to the senses, one goes to a terrible destination;
conquering these very senses, a man attains all desires.
The self-controlled man goes to heaven; the self-controlled man goes on the path of liberation;
for the self-controlled there is no grief at all; the self-controlled attains indeed all desires.

Thus ends the two hundred and sixty-fourth chapter, entitled 'Description of the Praise of Self-Control,' in the Haṃsagītā addressed to the sages, in the dialogue between Mārkaṇḍeya and Vajra, in the third book of the glorious Viṣṇudharmottara.
3.265
Haṃsa said:
Truth alone is the supreme brahman; truth alone is the supreme austerity;
truth alone is the supreme sacrifice; truth alone is the supreme sacred learning.
Truth keeps watch in the Vedas; release is the fruit of the tree of truth;
[so too are] austerity, renown, merit, and the worship of ancestors, gods, and seers.
The primordial ordinance and knowledge—everything is established upon truth;
truth is the sacrifices, as well as the Vedas, the mantras, and the goddess Sarasvatī.
So too the observance of vows is truth, and the syllable Oṃ is truth alone;
by truth the wind blows; by truth the sun shines;
by truth fire burns; by truth one goes to heaven;
and by truth Parjanya pours waters upon the surface of the earth.
Reciting the entirety of all the Vedas, bathing in all sacred fords,
and speaking the truth in the world—might these two be equal, or are they not equal?

A thousand horse-sacrifices and truth were held on a scale;
truth alone outweighs a thousand horse-sacrifices.
By truth the gods are pleased, as are the ancestors and brahmins;
they declare truth to be the highest dharma; therefore, one should not transgress truth.
The sages are devoted to truth; the foremost of the gods are established in truth;
always intent on truth, they rejoice in heaven like divinities.
And they travel in celestial chariots crowded with hosts of apsarases;
truth should indeed always be spoken; there is nothing higher than truth.
In the unfathomable, spotless, pure pool of purity whose water is truth,
one should bathe in the mental sacred ford; that bathing is considered supreme.
Whether for their own sake, or for another's sake, or for the sake of a son, or on account of an enemy,
those who do not speak untruth—those men go to heaven.
That which is exceedingly beneficial to living beings is truth—such is the conviction;
whatever would be beneficial to living beings, that very thing should be spoken by one who knows.

3.55.m:
On this subject also, the unfallen cite this ancient verse: / 'If you have no dispute with yourself and with King Yama, the son of Vivasvat, who dwells in your heart, / do not go to the Gaṅgā, nor go to Kurukṣetra.'
One should continually perform action that gives lightness to the mind; / all the meritorious deeds of such a person become manifold,
whichever [deeds] a mortal endowed with purity of mind performs. / The mind alone reveals the initial forms of a man;
the mind alone knows the heaviness and lightness of an action. / Truth is purity, self-restraint is purity, and bodily cleanliness is purity;
all purities belong to him whose mind is always pure; / for one bereft of mental purity, all purities are useless.
Yet it cannot be made artificially by those devoid of faithlessness.
He whose mind is ever free from defect and subject to virtue,
he alone sacrifices with sacrifices; he alone performs asceticism;
he alone attains all whatever he desires in his mind.
Therefore, mental purity is declared superior to all purities.

3.57.m:
