and turned upward, O foremost of the twice-born, placing the right above the left,
and the hands should be positioned with the thumbs slightly bent;
raising [the body] thereby and keeping the arms slightly loose without strain,
the wise man should align the spine with the shoulders and elevate the body;
the neck should be made motionless and very firm, and the head held level.
Looking at the tip of his nose and not looking at the directions,
closing the eyes slightly, he should cast off all that is external;
renouncing all that is external, [he should behold] the Supreme Self with the self,
in the heart-lotus, in the center of the pericarp, splendour deposited in splendour;
having beheld the Lord, the Cosmic Person, he is freed from all bonds.
Having first restrained the mind through withdrawal,
a man who has gained a target through concentration ascends to meditation.

Having conquered the senses, having controlled the mind,
and having likewise conquered the breath, a man ascends to meditation;
mounted on meditation, he attains that state having gone to which he grieves no more.
At the end of the night and at twilight, effort must be made by a man in meditation;
having seen the Supreme Cosmic Person through meditation, freed from the body, he attains wholeness.
3.284
The Haṃsa said:
Now, having mounted meditation, he should attain samādhi of the mind;
having attained samādhi, a man should not think of anything thereafter.

3.59.m:
By serving cows here, O chiefs of the twice-born, one attains worlds that are spotless and free from sorrow;
therefore, service to cows must be performed with effort by men intent on dharma.
Haṃsa said:
He who is compassionate is a true man; the pitiless one is considered a rākṣasa.
Even rākṣasas possess pity, O best of the twice-born.
The merciless go straight to a terrible hell;
then, in human birth, they suffer killing, imprisonment, and torment.
They become afflicted with illness, miserable, wretched, and poor;
[while] compassionate men, having reached heaven, shine brilliantly.
Therefore a wise man should be compassionate, even toward blades of grass;
even blades of grass are endowed with life; one should not inflict pain on them.
Compassion is to be shown to an insect, a winged creature, a domestic animal, and likewise to a deer;
to the highest class, to the one devoid of class, to an outcaste, and to one of mixed class.
Compassion is to be practiced everywhere; it grants the fruit of all desires;
even among all vows, the single vow of compassion is supreme.
